    <section class="slide title-slide active">
      <div class="badge">💧</div>
      <p class="stage">Stage 1</p>
      <h1>How the Philippines Meets Its Demand for Water</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Traditional water use, economic pressure, and a system that's running out of time.</p>
      <p class="byline">Add your group's names and section here</p>
    </section>

    <section class="slide">
      <p class="kicker">Part 01</p>
      <h2>Traditional uses of water</h2>
      <div class="two-column intro-grid">
        <p class="lead">Water touches nearly every hour of Filipino daily life: drinking and cooking, bathing and washing, and—in farming communities—irrigating the rice, corn, and vegetable plots that feed the family table and the local market.</p>
        <div class="icon-grid">
          <div><span>💧</span><strong>Drinking</strong></div>
          <div><span>🚿</span><strong>Hygiene</strong></div>
          <div><span>🍲</span><strong>Cooking</strong></div>
          <div><span>🌱</span><strong>Agriculture</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-band">
        <div class="stat"><strong>98.4%</strong><span>urban families</span></div>
        <div class="stat teal-stat"><strong>94.1%</strong><span>rural families</span></div>
        <p>have at least a basic level of drinking water service (2022). <small>[1]</small></p>
      </div>
      <span class="page-number">2 / 7</span>
    </section>

    <section class="slide">
      <p class="kicker">Part 02</p>
      <h2>Water as an economic good <span class="heading-badge">₱</span></h2>
      <p>Once water is scarce, it stops being background infrastructure and becomes something households and farmers actively budget for.</p>
      <div class="cards">
        <article class="card"><h3>Piped connection</h3><strong>₱320</strong><p>median monthly bill; ₱500 in Metro Manila. <small>[4]</small></p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Vendor or delivery truck</h3><strong class="clay">10×</strong><p>the price of piped water, in some areas. <small>[5]</small></p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="dark-band economic-band">
        <div><h3>On the farming side</h3><strong>80%+</strong><p>historic share of total national water use. <small>[6]</small></p></div>
        <div><strong>80% → 74%</strong><p>share of formal water permits going to irrigation, 2012 to 2022. <small>[7]</small></p></div>
      </div>
      <span class="page-number">3 / 7</span>
    </section>

    <section class="slide">
      <p class="kicker">Part 03</p>
      <h2>Coping with scarcity</h2>
      <div class="steps">
        <article><span>🕒</span><h3>Ration</h3><p>Metro Manila's water firms schedule service cuts of a few hours up to nearly a full day when reservoirs run low. <small>[8]</small></p></article>
        <article><span>🛠️</span><h3>Dig or deepen wells</h3><p>Falling back on groundwater when piped or surface supply can't keep up.</p></article>
        <article><span>🌱</span><h3>Change crops</h3><p>In 2015, NIA encouraged farmers to plant early-maturing, drought-tolerant rice to cut water use. <small>[9]</small></p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="comparison"><article><h3>City household</h3><p>Can usually still buy water from a truck or refilling station during a cut, even at a higher price.</p></article><article class="dark-band"><h3>Rural farmer</h3><p>With no deep well and a smaller harvest to sell, may have no fallback option at all.</p></article></div>
      <span class="page-number">4 / 7</span>
    </section>

    <section class="slide dark-slide">
      <p class="kicker">Part 04</p>
      <h2>Why the traditional methods are failing</h2>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <article><span>👥</span><strong>77M → 103M</strong><p>population growth, 2000–2016, as per-capita water fell from 1,907 to 1,400 m³ a year. <small>[10]</small></p></article>
        <article><span>🌡️</span><strong>1,300 m³</strong><p>per-capita water availability today—below the 1,700 m³ water-stress line. <small>[11]</small></p></article>
        <article><span>🌾</span><strong>700,000 MT</strong><p>possible rice production loss in a strong El Niño year. <small>[12]</small></p></article>
      </div>
      <hr>
      <p class="closing">Waiting for rain or digging a little deeper assumes the old pattern still holds. A growing population and a shifting climate mean it increasingly doesn't.</p>
      <span class="page-number">5 / 7</span>
    </section>

    <section class="slide">
      <div class="question-mark">?</div><h2>Guide questions</h2>
      <ol class="questions">
        <li>What are the main traditional uses of water in the Philippines, and how has stable access helped communities and farms grow?</li>
        <li>How do families and farmers manage when water is scarce?</li>
        <li>Who is most affected when water runs low, and how does this create inequality between people in the city and those in rural areas?</li>
        <li>Why is the traditional way of using water becoming unsustainable, and what new challenges make it harder to meet everyone's needs?</li>
      </ol>
      <span class="page-number">6 / 7</span>
    </section>

